BT /F1 10 Tf 56 771.5 Td (The bunkering station must be cleaned thoroughly to remove any residual oil or sludge. All) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 757.0 Td (tools, hoses, and equipment must be returned to their designated storage areas. The pre-) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 742.5 Td (transfer checklist and bunker delivery notes are to be filed in the official engine room) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 728.0 Td (records for future reference and audit purposes. A post-operation debrief may be conducted) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 713.5 Td (to discuss any operational issues or improvements for future bunkering events.) Tj ET
BT /F2 13 Tf 56 671.4 Td (Spill Response) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 642.4 Td (In the event of a fuel spill during bunkering, immediate action is required to minimize) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 627.9 Td (environmental impact and safety hazards. The primary rule is to stop the source of the spill) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 613.4 Td (immediately. If wind speed exceeds 30 knots or during thunderstorms, bunkering must be) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 598.9 Td (stopped if these conditions are detected or forecasted to worsen. These weather conditions) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 584.4 Td (pose significant risks to hose integrity and crew safety on deck.) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 555.4 Td (If a spill occurs, the duty engineer must activate the emergency stop mechanism. The SOPEP) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 540.9 Td (team must deploy absorbent materials and containment booms around the spill area to prevent) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 526.4 Td (it from reaching the scuppers or the sea. All non-essential personnel must be evacuated from) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 511.9 Td (the immediate danger zone. The Chief Engineer is responsible for reporting the incident to) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 497.4 Td (the Master and the company's designated person ashore, initiating the internal investigation) Tj ET
BT /F1 10 Tf 56 482.9 Td (process, and documenting the response actions taken.) Tj ET
BT /F1 8 Tf 56 28 Td (SMS-07 Bunkering Procedure \(fictional\) - page 3 of 3) Tj ET
